        },
        {
          "en": "One who sets fire to a stack. He kindled [a fire] on his own property, and it spread and consumed [a stack] on another person’s property. Rabbi Yehudah says: He pays for everything inside it. For Rabbi Yehudah imposes liability for damage to concealed things by fire. He does not accept the exposition of ‘or the standing grain’: just as standing grain is exposed, so [liability applies only to] anything exposed. The Sages say: He pays only for a stack, etc. For they accept the exposition of ‘or the standing grain’ and exempt [the kindler] from damage to concealed things by fire. However, they assess the space occupied by the utensils as though it were a stack, and he pays [the value of] a stack corresponding to the volume of the utensils themselves. From what they say in the concluding clause—‘The Sages agree with Rabbi Yehudah that one who sets fire to a building pays for everything inside it’—it is established in the Gemara that Rabbi Yehudah and the Sages also disagree about one who kindles [a fire] within another person’s property. Rabbi Yehudah holds that when he kindles [a fire] on another person’s property, he pays for everything inside it, even a purse. The Rabbis hold that he pays for utensils ordinarily concealed in a stack, such as threshing sledges and ox equipment, but does not pay for utensils not ordinarily concealed in a stack. The law is in accordance with the Sages. If a kid was tied to it, he is liable. For living creatures, too, are included by ‘or the standing grain.’ He cannot be exempted on the ground that the greater penalty takes precedence, for he does not incur a death penalty on account of the slave: since [the slave] was not tied, he should have fled, and [the kindler] is exempt on his account from both death and monetary payment. But if a slave was tied to it, he is exempt even for the kid and the stack, because he incurs a death penalty on account of the slave, and the greater penalty takes precedence. As for the kid, it makes no difference to us whether it was tied or untied; because [the teacher] mentioned [being tied] for the slave, he mentioned it for the kid. One who sets fire to a building. This is one who kindles [a fire] within another person’s property, and it is like destroying [the property] directly by hand. Even so, the reason [for liability for its contents] is that people ordinarily put utensils in houses. But in a stack, where people ordinarily put only such things as threshing sledges and ox equipment—even though he kindled [the fire] within another person’s property—according to the Sages he pays only for things ordinarily concealed in a stack.",
          "explanation": {
            "paragraphs": [
              {
                "en": "Bartenura distinguishes two settings. When fire spreads from the kindler’s property, the Sages exempt concealed utensils but value their occupied space as stack contents; Rabbi Yehudah imposes liability for concealed property. With direct kindling on the other person’s property, Rabbi Yehudah includes even a purse, whereas the Sages cover utensils ordinarily stored in a stack, such as threshing sledges and ox equipment. The ruling follows the Sages. In the slave-and-kid comment, the untied slave could flee, so the kindler is exempt on his account; the tied slave creates the capital liability that displaces monetary payment. Being tied makes no difference for the kid. A house normally contains utensils, explaining liability there. Even for direct kindling, the Sages retain the condition of customary storage.",
                "he": "הברטנורא מפריד שתי זירות: באש שיצאה מרשותו, החכמים פוטרים על כלים טמונים אבל מחשבים את מקומם כתכולת הגדיש; רבי יהודה מחייב גם על הטמון. כשהצית בתוך של חברו, רבי יהודה מחייב אף על ארנקי, והחכמים מחייבים רק על כלים שדרכם להיות בגדיש, כגון מוריגין וכלי בקר; ההלכה כחכמים. בדיבור על העבד והגדי העבד שאינו כפות היה יכול לברוח, ולכן אין חיוב עליו, ואילו עבד כפות יוצר את חיוב המיתה הדוחה תשלומים. לגבי הגדי אין חילוק בכפיתה. בבית חייבים על הכלים מפני שדרכם להיות שם, וגם בהצתה ישירה נשמר לפי החכמים התנאי של דרך ההנחה."
              }
