      },
      "units": [
        {
          "en": "Rabbi Shmuel bar Nachmani said in the name of Rabbi Yonasan: Calamity comes to the world only when wicked people are in the world, yet it begins first with the righteous, as it is said: ‘When fire goes out and finds thorns.’ When does fire go out? When thorns are available for it. Yet it begins first with the righteous, as it is said: ‘And a stack of grain has been consumed.’ It does not say ‘and it consumes a stack of grain,’ but rather ‘and a stack of grain has been consumed’—the stack of grain has already been consumed.\n\nRav Yehuda said in the name of Rav: A person should always enter [his lodging] while it is ‘good’ [daylight], and leave while it is ‘good,’ as it is said: ‘And you, none of you shall go out of the entrance of his house until morning.’\n\nThe Rabbis taught: If there is pestilence in the city, draw in your feet [and stay inside], as it is said: ‘And you, none of you shall go out of the entrance of his house until morning.’ And it says: ‘Go, My people, enter your chambers and shut your doors behind you.’ And it says: ‘Outside, the sword shall bereave, and within the chambers, terror.’\n\nWhy [add] ‘And it says’? If you were to say that these words apply at night but not by day, come and hear: ‘Go, My people, enter your chambers and shut your doors.’ And if you were to say that these words apply [where] there is no terror inside, but where there is terror inside it would be better to go out and sit among people, in company, come and hear: ‘Outside, the sword shall bereave, and within the chambers, terror.’ Even though there is terror within the chambers, outside the sword shall bereave.\n\nDuring a time of wrath, Rava would close the windows, for it is written: ‘For death has come up through our windows.’\n\nThe Rabbis taught: If there is famine in the city, scatter your feet [and leave], as it is said: ‘There was famine in the land, and Avram went down to Egypt [to sojourn] (and he sojourned) there.’ And it says: ‘If we say: Let us enter the city, famine is in the city, and we shall die there.’ Why [add] ‘And it says’? If you were to say that these words apply where there is no possible danger to life [at the destination], but not where there is possible danger to life, come and hear: ‘Let us go and fall into the camp of Aram; if they let us live, we shall live.’\n\nThe Rabbis taught: If there is pestilence in the city, a person should not walk in the middle of the road, because the angel of death walks in the middle of the roads: Since permission has been granted to him, he walks openly. If there is peace in the city, one should not walk at the sides of the roads: Since he has no permission, he goes about in concealment.\n\nThe Rabbis taught: If there is pestilence in the city, a person should not enter the synagogue alone, because the angel of death deposits his implements there. These words apply where children do not study there and ten [men] do not pray there.\n\nThe Rabbis taught: If dogs are weeping, the angel of death has come to the city. If dogs are playing, Eliyahu the prophet has come to the city.",
          "explanation": {
            "paragraphs": [
              {
                "en": "The passage opens with an exposition of fire and thorns: the wicked are represented by thorns, while the passive wording “a stack has been consumed” is read as indicating that the stack, representing the righteous, was consumed first. The discussion then presents precautions with their scriptural proofs. “While it is good” is connected with daylight, and pestilence in the city calls for staying inside. The successive additional verses answer two proposed limitations: perhaps only at night, or perhaps fear inside justifies going out. The account of Rava closes this group of teachings about the house and windows. Famine presents a contrasting case for departure. The four afflicted men’s proposal to enter the camp of Aram extends the argument beyond a safe destination to one involving possible danger to life. The road teaching then contrasts the middle during pestilence with the sides during peace, within its description of the angel of death. The warning about entering a synagogue alone is expressly conditioned on the absence of both children studying and ten men praying. The final statements about dogs’ weeping and playing must retain the immediately following qualification that no female dog is among them. These are the classical passage’s teachings and expositions, not medical diagnosis of an outbreak or a definitive interpretation of particular dogs’ behavior. The actual passage is in Bava Kamma 60a–60b, not the location named in the daily heading. The quoted forms לגור and ויגר are alternatives, not two successive actions.",
                "he": "הקטע פותח בדרשה על אש וקוצים: הרשעים נמשלים לקוצים, והלשון הסבילה ״ונאכל גדיש״ נדרשת כאילו הגדיש, המייצג את הצדיקים, כבר נאכל בתחילה. אחר כך מובאות הנהגות זהירות והפסוקים המבססים אותן. ״בכי טוב״ נקשר לאור היום, ודבר בעיר מצריך כניסה פנימה; רצף ״ואומר״ משיב לשתי הסתייגויות, שמא רק בלילה ושמא פחד מבפנים מצדיק יציאה. הסיפור על רבא סוגר את קבוצת הדברים על הבית והחלונות. רעב מציב מקרה שונה של יציאה, והפסוק בארבעת המצורעים מרחיב את הדיון גם כשיש ספק נפשות ביעד, לא רק יעד בטוח. ההמשך מבחין בין דרך האמצע בזמן דבר לצדי הדרך בזמן שלום בתוך תיאור מלאך המוות. האזהרה על כניסה יחיד לבית הכנסת מותנית בכך שאין בו לימוד ילדים ואין בו תפילת עשרה. הסיום מתאר בכיית כלבים ומשחקם; התנאי המופיע מיד אחר הקטע, שאין ביניהם כלבה, נשאר חיוני להבנת הדברים. אלו תיאורים ודרשות של הסוגיה הקלאסית, לא אבחון רפואי של מגפה או פירוש מחייב להתנהגות כלבים במקרה מסוים. גוף הקטע הוא בבבא קמא ס ע״א–ע״ב, ולא במקום שהכותרת היומית מציינת. החלופות ״לגור״ ו״ויגר״ הן שתי קריאות בציטוט ולא שני מעשים."
              }
            ],
            "title": {
